---
source: crates/ym-cli/tests/cli.rs
expression: "help(&[\"render\"])"
---
Develop and export photos. Defaults match the app: automatic development and the default look. Options change them for this run only

Usage: ym render [OPTIONS] <FILES_OR_FOLDERS>...

Options:
  -q, --quiet
          Print only errors on stderr

  -v, --verbose
          Print what is happening on stderr: stages, timings and notes

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Photos:
      --recursive
          Walk the subfolders of folders too

  <FILES_OR_FOLDERS>...
          Files or folders to render; `-` reads file paths from stdin, one per line. Folders are walked for `*.ARW` (any case)

Look and display:
      --look <LOOK>
          Look: its full id (`builtin:neutral`), the part after the colon (`neutral`) or its name, in any case

      --intensity <PERCENT>
          How strongly the look applies, in percent (default 100)

      --display <aces2|arri>
          Display rendering: `aces2`, or `arri` (an imported ARRI display LUT: the one chosen in the app, else the only one imported; or give its id)

      --settings <FILE>
          A JSON file of settings (the `ym:Settings` schema), or `-` for stdin. It covers what these options can't: colour balance (`{ "kind": "auto" }` puts a shadows or highlights wheel back to Auto), curves, crop, orientation, white pick

Light:
      --exposure <auto|EV>
          Exposure in stops (-4 to 4), or `auto`

      --exposure-offset <EV>
          Stops added to each photo's automatic exposure

      --contrast <auto|N>
          Contrast, -100 to 100, or `auto` (the default: 0, or the value Apple Auto chose). Around mid grey; black and the brightest tones stay where they are

      --brightness <N>
          Brightness, -100 to 100

      --highlights <auto|N>
          Highlights, -100 to 100, or `auto` (the default: 0, or the value Apple Auto chose)

      --shadows <auto|N>
          Shadows, -100 to 100, or `auto` (the default: 0, or the value Apple Auto chose)

      --white <N>
          White point, -100 to 100

      --black <auto|N>
          Black point, -100 to 100, or `auto` (the default: 0, or the value Apple Auto chose)

      --white-offset <EV>
          Stops added to each photo's automatic white lift, -3 to 3. 0 is the automatic amount; an offset that takes the lift to 0 or below switches it off (`--white-offset -3` always does). The lift only acts with the built-in ACES 2 display rendering, and a DNG carries it

Colour:
      --wb <auto|K[,TINT]>
          White balance: `auto`, or a temperature in kelvin (2000 to 11000) with an optional tint (-50 to 50), as `5200` or `5200,4`

      --light <LIGHT>
          Light preset instead of the estimated light

          Possible values:
          - auto:        The estimated light (clears a preset from `--settings`)
          - day:         Daylight, 5500 K
          - shade:       Shade, 7500 K
          - tungsten:    Tungsten, 3200 K
          - led:         LED light at the estimated colour
          - fluorescent: Fluorescent light at the estimated colour

      --saturation <auto|N>
          Saturation, -100 to 100, or `auto` (the default: 0, or the value Apple Auto chose). More makes colours stronger, less makes them greyer; brightness stays

Detail:
      --nr <auto|L,C,D>
          Noise reduction: `auto`, or luminance, colour and detail strengths (0 to 100 each)

      --sharpen <auto|AMOUNT,RADIUS>
          Sharpening: `auto`, or amount (0 to 300) and radius in pixels (0.3 to 2.5). A DNG is never sharpened

      --output-sharpening <PERCENT>
          Output sharpening, 0 to 200 percent (default 100, 0 is off). It sharpens JPEG, TIFF and PNG files at the size they are written, after the resize; a DNG is never sharpened

RAW:
      --no-neural
          Use the classical demosaic instead of the learned one

      --no-highlight-recovery
          Switch highlight reconstruction off

      --no-lens
          Switch the lens corrections (vignetting, distortion) off

      --no-ca
          Switch the chromatic-aberration correction off

      --rolloff <HIGHLIGHTS,LOW-LIGHT>
          Highlight range and low-light toe, 0 to 1 each, as `1,0.5` (the defaults). Highlights: where the camera's clipping point lands, from where it was recorded (0) to white, the clip of an ALEXA at EI 800 (1)

      --profile <ID>
          Force a camera profile, for comparisons (for example `sony-ilce-7rm4`)

Output:
      --format <FORMAT>
          File format (default: the format chosen in the app, else jpeg)

          Possible values:
          - jpeg: JPEG
          - tiff: TIFF
          - png:  PNG
          - dng:  DNG with the look baked in. Apps other than Adobe's show the stored linear image darker until their own exposure handling is applied. With a look that includes its own display rendering, the DNG is close to, not the same as, the picture you see

      --quality <N>
          JPEG quality, 50 to 100 (default 92)

      --bits <BITS>
          Bits per channel for TIFF and PNG, 8 or 16 (default 16)

      --compression <METHOD>
          TIFF compression (default lzw)

          Possible values:
          - none: No compression
          - lzw:  LZW
          - zip:  Deflate, shown as ZIP

      --colour-space <SPACE>
          Colour space of JPEG, TIFF and PNG files (default srgb)

          Possible values:
          - srgb:    sRGB
          - p3:      Display P3
          - rec2020: Rec.2020
          
          [alias: --color-space]

      --long-edge <PX>
          Scale JPEG, TIFF and PNG files so the long edge has this many pixels (never enlarges)

      --out <DIR>
          Folder for the exports (default: `<source folder> export`)

      --name <TEMPLATE>
          File name template with {name}, {look}, {date}, {seq} and {camera} (default `{name}_{look}`)

      --skip-existing
          Leave a file that already exists alone (default: keep both, adding " 2", " 3")

      --overwrite
          Replace a file that already exists

Run:
      --jobs <N>
          Photos read and analysed at the same time. The export itself renders one photo at a time

      --json
          Print one JSON object per file on stdout, as each file finishes

      --require-gpu
          Stop with exit code 4 when there is no usable GPU, instead of using the CPU

      --cpu
          Render on the CPU only and open no graphics device. The golden images are made this way. Beats YELLOWMOON_GPU

      --apple-auto <on|off>
          Apple Auto for this run. On a Mac with Apple Intelligence, Apple's on-device model grades each photo: it sets the automatic exposure, highlights, shadows, black, contrast, warmth, tint and saturation and the colours of the shadows and highlights. Default: the app's setting where Apple Auto is available, off elsewhere

      --apple-auto-instructions <FILE>
          A text file with instructions for Apple's model, used for this run instead of those in Preferences (at most 2000 characters; an empty file is the default instructions)

Examples:
  ym render ~/Pictures/2026-10-04 --look neutral --format jpeg --long-edge 3000 --out ~/Exports
  fd -e ARW . /Volumes/A1 | ym render - --json --format tiff --bits 16 > results.jsonl
  ym render DSC04204.ARW --exposure-offset -0.5 --wb 5200,4

Per-photo settings beyond these options go in a JSON file given with --settings (the `ym:Settings` schema of an exported file). Options given alongside it override it. A file with several photos' settings is an object keyed by file name.
